  "comment": "kiln.ui's chat history (GitHub issue 44, phase 1) on the client: Up/Down in the game's chat field (client 26430625, enshrouded.exe 5d9ef2f1). Evidence: docs/re/chat-input.md. TextInputProcess(widget, &result, &label, mode) runs one text field for one UI frame on the UI thread; it writes result {u32 event (0 none, 1 Enter, 2 Escape), u8} and returns &result in rax. The HUD chat (HudChatWindow) and the Social tab (SocialChat) each call it once: Kiln acts only on those two calls, told apart by their return addresses HudInputReturn and SocialInputReturn. The field's widget keeps the caller's chat string at widget+widget.buffer: a pointer to {char* data, u64 length, u64 capacity} (UTF-8, capacity 0x400), the very bytes the caller sends on Enter. TextInputState(widget, state.hash, 1) returns the field's persistent state block (the UI's hash-keyed state store, 0x1990 bytes, freed when the field is not drawn for a frame); its byte state+state.editing is set while the field has typing focus, and state+state.edit is the field's editor. The editor's own text is UTF-32 (one u32 per character, length in characters), so Kiln never reads it. EditSetText(&result, editor, &{data, length}) is the game's own replace-the-text (UTF-8 in; clears the undo history, cursor and selection to the end); the chat calls it with an empty string after sending. Its result is 8 bytes {u8 error, u32 at +4}; error 0 is success. TextInputEdit is TextInputProcess's editing callee, where the layout constants are captured.",
